;;; undefined_variable_refusal_test.esk
;;;
;;; This program is INTENDED to fail to compile. It exists to pin the shape of
;;; the refusal, not the diagnosis.
;;;
;;; A name that really is undefined must produce a diagnostic and exit 1 on
;;; every execution axis. The in-process JIT (`ESHKOL_JIT_CACHE=0 -r`) used to
;;; exit 139 instead: the JIT-execution gate in ReplJITContext::addModule threw
;;; to refuse the module, and the unwind destroyed the rejected module's
;;; LLVMContext before the Module itself, so `llvm::Module::~Module` reached
;;; `LLVMContext::removeModule` through freed memory. The compiler crashed
;;; while correctly rejecting the program.
;;;
;;; That is how LE-01 read as a memory-safety defect: `(sort xs string<?)`
;;; raised "Undefined variable: string<?" — itself a bug, fixed separately —
;;; and the axis with the cache off then reported SIGSEGV rather than the
;;; diagnostic that had already been printed.
(define (call-it f a) (f a))
(display (call-it no-such-variable-anywhere 1))
